_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01BaETAqpLJQZmkaxnDUaXWg", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api"}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled payments-worker back to v3.3.0 and opened the SEV2 incident. payments-worker is healthy again, but payments-api is still unhealthy and someone needs to look at it.\n\n**What I did:**\n1. There's no service called just \"payments.\" I found **payments-api** first: it's unhealthy on v5.1.0, with an 18% error rate. I tried to roll it back to v5.0.0, its last healthy release, but the approver denied it: \"Wrong service: the problem is in payments-worker.\" So I left payments-api on v5.1.0.\n2. **payments-worker** had v3.4.0 in production, marked unhealthy, with an 18% error rate in us-east, us-west and eu-west. I **rolled it back to v3.3.0**, its most recent release that was healthy in production. It's now live in all three regions and the error rate is down to 0.2%.\n3. **I opened the SEV2 incident** for payments-worker, which pages on-call. The system reported it as created. The summary covers the rollback and says payments-api is still unhealthy.\n\n**Still open:** payments-api is still on v5.1.0, unhealthy, with an 18% error rate. Fixing payments-worker didn't bring it back. Either its own release (marked degraded) is causing the errors, or it needs more time to recover. I didn't roll it back because the approver denied that. If its errors don't drop soon, someone should look at it separately, and the fix may be rolling it back to v5.0.0 after all.", "type": "text"}]}]}